Bundled "globally trusted" signing certificates — EU Trusted Lists (EUTL).
=========================================================================

STATUS: EMPTY. This file holds no certificates yet.

An empty list is a safe state, not a broken one. It means nothing is trusted
"globally", so a signature is trusted only when the user has added the signer's
certificate themselves. That is the app's main trust path anyway. No signature
is ever shown as trusted because of anything in this file today.

Everything outside the BEGIN/END CERTIFICATE markers is ignored by the parser
(see lib/features/signature/data/eutl_trust_list.dart), which is why this note
can live here.


How to populate it
------------------

The EUTL is published by the European Commission as a "List of Trusted Lists"
(LOTL) in XML, which points to one trusted list per member state. Filling this
file means fetching those, pulling out the signing certificates, and appending
them below in PEM form:

    -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
    MIIF...base64...
    -----END CERTIFICATE-----

Do this deliberately and record it. When you add certificates, write down here:

  - the exact source URL they came from,
  - the date they were fetched,
  - how the download itself was verified (the LOTL is signed — check it).


Two things to know before you do
--------------------------------

1. THIS LIST CANNOT REFRESH ITSELF. The app is offline by rule and has no
   INTERNET permission, so whatever is committed here is frozen until someone
   ships a new version of the app. A certificate withdrawn from the EUTL after
   that would still be trusted here. That is the trade for being offline, and it
   is the reason this list is a convenience rather than the backbone.

2. ADOBE'S AATL DOES NOT GO IN THIS FILE. Adobe's Approved Trust List is
   distributed through Adobe's own updater and its redistribution terms are not
   clearly open. Project rule 1 says check the licence before adding anything, so
   AATL stays out until someone confirms the terms allow bundling it.
